1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,742
Konnichiwa. This is Liam, in for Bear. Installing a plugin takes one command. So the

2
00:00:04,762 --> 00:00:08,869
question isn't how to install one. It's how to look inside it first.

3
00:00:08,939 --> 00:00:13,907
Three terms. The manifest: a file called plugin dot json that names the plugin. A

4
00:00:13,927 --> 00:00:18,895
hook: a command that runs by itself when something happens in your session. And an

5
00:00:18,915 --> 00:00:22,885
MCP server: a program that plugs Claude into outside tools and data.

6
00:00:22,955 --> 00:00:27,833
Here's the plugin box from last time. On disk, a plugin is just a folder. Open it,

7
00:00:27,853 --> 00:00:30,714
and each kind of part has its own place inside.

8
00:00:30,784 --> 00:00:36,161
First, the shipping label: a file called plugin dot json, in a folder named dot

9
00:00:36,181 --> 00:00:40,478
claude-plugin. It holds the plugin's name, a description, and sometimes a version.

10
00:00:40,498 --> 00:00:42,277
Only the name is required.

11
00:00:42,347 --> 00:00:47,112
Slash commands are the parts you trigger yourself. Each one is a markdown file in

12
00:00:47,132 --> 00:00:51,579
the commands folder. Type slash commit, and Claude follows the steps written in that

13
00:00:51,599 --> 00:00:51,898
file.

14
00:00:51,968 --> 00:00:56,982
Sub-agents are helpers with one job, and each one is a markdown file in the agents

15
00:00:57,002 --> 00:01:01,701
folder. Claude hands one a multi-step task. It works through it on its own, then

16
00:01:01,721 --> 00:01:02,330
reports back.

17
00:01:02,400 --> 00:01:07,635
Skills are folders of know-how, each with a SKILL.md file. You don't have to type

18
00:01:07,655 --> 00:01:11,840
anything. Claude reads each skill's description, and opens one when the task

19
00:01:11,860 --> 00:01:12,190
matches.

20
00:01:12,260 --> 00:01:17,442
Hooks fire on events. The hooks file says: when Claude edits a file, or finishes a

21
00:01:17,462 --> 00:01:21,669
turn, run this command. The security-guidance plugin uses hooks to check each edit

22
00:01:21,689 --> 00:01:25,246
for risky code. That's a real program, running on your machine.

23
00:01:25,316 --> 00:01:30,965
MCP servers are listed in dot mcp dot json. They plug Claude into outside tools and

24
00:01:30,985 --> 00:01:36,279
data, and they start when the plugin is turned on. GitHub's plugin holds one entry:

25
00:01:36,299 --> 00:01:39,113
GitHub's own server, reached with your access token.

26
00:01:39,183 --> 00:01:44,302
And a plugin carries only the parts it needs. Commit-commands is a label and three

27
00:01:44,322 --> 00:01:48,414
slash commands. GitHub's is a label and one MCP server. Nothing else.

28
00:01:48,484 --> 00:01:53,103
Plugins live in a marketplace, a catalog you install from. Anthropic's official one

29
00:01:53,123 --> 00:01:58,099
lists two hundred fifty-five. But the catalog's own README says: make sure you trust

30
00:01:58,119 --> 00:02:02,025
a plugin before you install it. Anthropic doesn't control what's inside.

31
00:02:02,095 --> 00:02:06,238
Your turn. Paste this into Claude Code: Before I install the Claude Code plugin

32
00:02:06,258 --> 00:02:10,104
[name], read its folder. List every part it ships: plugin dot json, commands,

33
00:02:10,124 --> 00:02:14,268
sub-agents, skills, hooks, and MCP servers. For each hook, give the event and the

34
00:02:14,288 --> 00:02:19,026
exact command it runs. For each MCP server, say where it connects and what keys it

35
00:02:19,046 --> 00:02:23,487
needs. Then list what it could read, change, or send off my machine. Don't install

36
00:02:23,507 --> 00:02:27,948
or run anything. Then check two things yourself. Open the hooks file and read every

37
00:02:27,968 --> 00:02:32,706
command it runs. And open dot mcp dot json. Does every server match what the plugin

38
00:02:32,726 --> 00:02:33,598
says it does?

39
00:02:33,668 --> 00:02:38,203
What's Inside a Plugin Folder. At Nik Bear Brown.
